    {
      "sample_index": 1,
      "text": "To find the area of the square, we need to first determine the length of its side. According to the problem, the length of the side of the square can be expressed in two different ways: \\(4x - 15\\) meters and \\(20 - 3x\\) meters. Since both expressions represent the same length, we can set them equal to each other and solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n4x - 15 = 20 - 3x\n\\]\n\nNext, we will solve this equation for \\(x\\):\n\n1. Add \\(3x\\) to both sides of the equation:\n\\[\n4x + 3x - 15 = 20\n\\]\n\n2. Simplify the left side:\n\\[\n7x - 15 = 20\n\\]\n\n3. Add 15 to both sides to isolate the term with \\(x\\):\n\\[\n7x = 35\n\\]\n\n4. Divide both sides by 7 to solve for \\(x\\):\n\\[\nx = 5\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(x = 5\\), we can substitute this value back into either expression for the side length to find the actual length of the side of the square. We'll use \\(4x - 15\\):\n\n\\[\n4(5) - 15 = 20 - 15 = 5 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\n\nSo, the length of the side of the square is 5 meters. To find the area of the square, we square the length of the side:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = 5^2 = 25 \\text{ square meters}\n\\]\n\nLet's confirm this with Python code to ensure accuracy.\n```python\n# Define the expressions for the side length of the square\ndef side_length(x):\n    return 4*x - 15\n\n# Solve for x\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\nx = symbols('x')\nequation = Eq(4*x - 15, 20 - 3*x)\nsolution = solve(equation, x)[0]\n\n# Calculate the side length using the solution for x\nside_length_value = side_length(solution)\n\n# Calculate the area of the square\narea = side_length_value**2\nprint((solution, side_length_value, area))\n```\n```output\n(5, 5, 25)\n```\nThe solution for \\(x\\) is 5, the side length of the square is 5 meters, and the area of the square is \\(5^2 = 25\\) square meters. Therefore, the area of the square is \\(\\boxed{25}\\) square meters.",
