      "sample_index": 6,
      "text": "To find the area of the square, we first need to determine the length of one of its sides. Given that the length of one side is expressed as both \\(4x - 15\\) meters and \\(20 - 3x\\) meters, we can set these two expressions equal to each other and solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n4x - 15 = 20 - 3x\n\\]\n\nNext, we'll solve for \\(x\\):\n\n\\[\n4x + 3x = 20 + 15\n\\]\n\n\\[\n7x = 35\n\\]\n\n\\[\nx = 5\n\\]\n\nNow that we have the value of \\(x\\), we can substitute it back into either expression to find the length of one side of the square. Let's use \\(4x - 15\\):\n\n\\[\n4(5) - 15 = 20 - 15 = 5\n\\]\n\nSo, the length of one side of the square is 5 meters. The area of a square is given by the square of the length of its side:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = 5^2 = 25 \\text{ square meters}\n\\]\n\nLet's verify this calculation using Python code.\n```python\n# Given expressions for the side of the square\nfrom sympy import symbols, Eq, solve\n\n# Define the variable\nx = symbols('x')\n\n# Define the equations\nside1 = 4*x - 15\nside2 = 20 - 3*x\n\n# Set the equations equal to each other\nequation = Eq(side1, side2)\n\n# Solve for x\nsolution = solve(equation, x)\nx_value = solution[0]\n\n# Calculate the length of one side of the square\nside_length = side1.subs(x, x_value)\n\n# Calculate the area of the square\narea = side_length**2\nprint(area)\n```\n```output\n25\n```\nThe area of the square is \\(\\boxed{25}\\) square meters.",
